WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.140
Orders, costing and money records for tailors.

2
00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.550
This is the video for module 2 of Fashion Business Management.

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:16.220
Late outfits, lost measurements and arguments over balances usually come from the same root: no fixed process.

4
00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:24.940
When every order goes through the same stages, and every stage leaves a written record, you can take more orders with less stress.

5
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:34.510
In this module you also learn to turn a real cost into a price, and to keep a simple cash book so you always know where the money is.

6
00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:44.330
Consult. Agree the neckline, sleeve, length, lining and closures, and check her fabric is enough. Save the style picture in her chat.

7
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:53.370
Measure in the same order every time, tape snug but not tight, and write the date beside every set, because bodies change.

8
00:00:54.620 --> 00:01:01.730
Take a deposit that covers at least the materials you must buy, and give a receipt showing the amount paid and the balance.

9
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:10.460
Schedule the sewing into a specific week, then invite her for a try-on before final finishing, and note every adjustment.

10
00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:17.880
Press, check, and photograph the finished piece. Collect the balance, and only then release the garment.

11
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:23.630
Send your terms on WhatsApp before cutting. When she replies OK, you have a written record.

12
00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:36.590
Promise dates from your capacity, not from hope. For example, Funmi sews about 30 hours a week in her Ibadan shop. A client wants a lined gown by Saturday of next week.

13
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:45.650
Her order book shows only about 4 free hours next week. The new gown needs about 8, so it does not fit without overtime.

14
00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:56.840
So she offers an honest choice: delivery in two weeks at the normal price, or an express charge that pays a trained helper. The client chooses two weeks.

15
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:10.760
Now costing. First find your overhead rate. Zainab's running costs are about 100,000 naira a month, and she sews about 200 hours, so overheads are 500 naira an hour.

16
00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:20.590
Her materials are fabric at about 15,000 naira, lining at 3,000 and trims at 2,000. That is 20,000 naira.

17
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:31.030
The gown takes 8 hours. Labour at 1,000 naira an hour is 8,000, and overheads at 500 an hour are 4,000.

18
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:38.720
Total cost is 32,000 naira. This is her floor price. Anything below it loses money.

19
00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:46.450
For a 25% margin she divides by 0.75, and rounds to 43,000 naira.

20
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:53.340
With a full package, Zainab supplies everything, so the price must cover all the materials.

21
00:02:54.590 --> 00:03:02.880
With a sewing fee, she supplies only the trims. Her cost is about 14,000, so she charges about 19,000.

22
00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:11.280
If the shop next door charges 12,000, she cannot match it without losing money. She competes on fit and finish instead.

23
00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:17.750
If a client wants a lower price, change the order, not your margin: a simpler style, or no lining.

24
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:25.590
Open a separate account for the business. Your bank statement then becomes a second record of business money.

25
00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:37.940
Set your deposit so that, if a client disappears, you have lost nothing. You cannot resell fabric cut to one person's size. Apply this to friends and church members too.

26
00:03:39.190 --> 00:03:48.000
And stop taking money from the drawer for every need at home. Pay yourself a fixed wage, and write it in the cash book the same day as everything else.

27
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:53.340
At month end, total your cash book. Esther's figures are approximate.

28
00:03:54.590 --> 00:04:07.930
She took in 480,000 naira. She spent 190,000 on materials, 60,000 on her helper and 100,000 on overheads. That is 350,000 in costs.

29
00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:21.310
Her profit before her own wage is 130,000 naira. Her balances owed list shows 70,000 naira still to collect. Now she knows her real earnings instead of guessing.

30
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:28.280
Some fraudsters send fake credit alerts or edited screenshots, then rush to collect a garment.

31
00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:39.040
Confirm the money has arrived in your banking app or bank statement before you release anything. And never share your PIN, password or one-time code with anyone.

32
00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:51.340
To remember. Use the same stages for every order. Promise only what your hours allow. Cost every style and never quote below cost. And keep your cash book every day.

33
00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:58.690
Your turn. Set up your order book, cost your best-selling style, and keep a cash book for 14 days.

34
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:00.990
Continue with the lessons below.
